{"name":"Ube","hex":"#8878c3","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ube","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The purple yam, Dioscorea alata."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ube"}],"entries":[{"title":"UBE Corporation","blocks":[{"text":"UBE Corporation  is a Japanese chemical company manufacturing chemicals, plastics, battery materials, pharmaceuticals, cement, construction materials, and machinery."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UBE_Corporation","wikidata":"Q260121"},{"title":"Dioscorea alata","blocks":[{"text":"Dioscorea alata – also called ube, ubi, uwhi, purple yam, winged yam, or greater yam, among many other names – is a species of yam. The tubers are usually a vivid violet-purple to bright lavender, but some range from creamy-white to plain white. Although it is sometimes confused with taro and the Okinawa sweet potato beniimo , D. alata is also grown in Okinawa. Its origins are in the Asian and Oceanian tropics. Some varieties attain great size. A mambatap greater yam grown in Maprik, East Sepik Province, Papua New Guinea, around 1939 was 3.5 m (11 ft) in length."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Kambar_wh.jpg/330px-Kambar_wh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioscorea_alata","wikidata":"Q1932852"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg/250px-ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg/330px-ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg/250px-ANA_Hotel_Ube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AANA_Ho